    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate focusing on high-ceiling matchups against soft-tossing righties. **Vinnie Pasquantino** (76.21) headlines our attack, benefiting from an elite matchup against Gallen and a promotion to the #3 spot. Similarly, **Trevor Story** (73.82) is a primary start; the wind blowing out at his venue, paired with a favorable 5.22 xERA from the opposing pitcher, inflates his value significantly. **Mickey Moniak** (68.53) and **James Wood** (67.81) also project as high-efficiency anchors, with **James Wood** specifically thriving due to his elite 0.419 xwOBA and the leadoff protection that stabilizes his counting stat output.\n\nWhile most of the roster benefits from positive platoon splits, **Jose Caballero** remains our primary SB risk. Despite a respectable projection, his #9 spot in the order and a negative dynamic platoon adjustment drag his score down to 55.35, though his speed makes him a necessary evil in our hunt for points. Our most difficult decision on the bench was **Cole Young** (51.76). He was the final player cut, falling just short of **Travis Bazzana** (57.55) due to the latter\u2019s favorable park factors and the higher offensive environment associated with the #5 spot versus **Cole Young**\u2019s bottom-third lineup placement.\n\nWe are exercising caution with **Seiya Suzuki** (45.59) today; facing a pitcher with a 3.08 xERA presents a significant hurdle, and his score reflects that lack of ceiling. We have also opted to bench **Austin Riley** (48.57) and **Isaac Paredes** (44.90), as neither could overcome their respective matchups against tough arms or poor lineup positioning. We are keeping a close watch on **Corbin Carroll**, who remains unavailable, but we remain confident that our primary starters\u2014backed by the aggressive deployment of **Josh Naylor** and **Max Muncy**\u2014provide the best statistical path to clawing back ground in the standings today.",
